Backslash spellings of a protocol-relative destination, all of which a browser
folds to //evil.example because the URL standard treats a backslash as a
separator for special schemes:

[forward](//evil.example/a) and [mixed](/\evil.example/b) and
[reverse](\/evil.example/c) and [double](\\evil.example/d).

![image](/\evil.example/pixel.png)

Same-shape destinations that are NOT protocol-relative and must survive:
[rooted](/rooted/path), [dotted](./relative), [inner](/a\b).
